// ASan battery fixture for increment-5 (split-frontier drop flags): the
// CONDITIONAL-ALLOCATION half of the design (as opposed to
// split_frontier_loop.bld's conditional-USE half). `s` is allocated only
// when `i % 2 == 0` (half the iterations skip allocation entirely: the def
// block does not dominate the loop's exit), and the arm that DOES allocate
// immediately splits again on `i % 4 == 0`, using `s` on BOTH of that inner
// `if`'s branches with a different message.
//
// VERIFIED DEVIATION FROM THE DESIGN DOC'S ORIGINAL MOTIVATING FIXTURE:
// the design doc's first draft allocated and used `s` on a SINGLE arm (`if
// i < 1 { let s = ...; println!("{}", s); }`, no further split), framed as
// the "would-be UNINITIALIZED-FREE shape: an unguarded join free would free
// garbage `cap` on the skip path". Compiling that program and diffing
// flag-on against flag-off showed increment 4 (`multi_block_freeable`)
// already claims it, UNGUARDED -- and correctly so: because a `Call`
// terminator's continuation block is a single-predecessor site entirely
// inside the allocating arm's own dominance region (see
// split_frontier_loop.bld's header for the full mechanism), the free never
// merges with the skip path before running, so there is no uninitialized
// free to guard against in that exact program. The hazard the design doc
// describes is real for the STATIC placement rule (a naive free at the
// post-merge join, which is what `analysis::flags`'s own
// `conditional_alloc_enrolls_with_no_dominance` unit test constructs and
// verifies directly against MIR), but that exact join is not where real
// `if`/`else`-without-further-branching source lowers its free to.
//
// As with split_frontier_loop.bld, the shape that genuinely defeats
// increment 4 here is MULTIPLE candidate sites for the same conditionally-
// allocated owner: the inner `if i % 4 == 0` gives `s` two independently
// valid single-predecessor continuation blocks (one per branch), so
// `multi_block_freeable`'s function-wide uniqueness requirement declines
// the owner entirely. Increment 5 claims both sites (no uniqueness
// requirement, no dominance requirement -- `def_bi` need not dominate a
// site, which matters here since `s`'s def is nested two `if`s deep) plus
// the guarded Return backstop for the iterations that never allocate at
// all. This is still the would-be UNINITIALIZED-FREE shape in spirit (the
// `i % 2 != 0` iterations never allocate `s`, and the guard is what keeps
// the shared flag's Return-backstop free from firing on those iterations'
// eventual function exit); the ASan mutation run recorded in the design
// doc (flipping the flag's initializer from `= 0` to `= 1`) proves the
// guard is load-bearing.
//
// See docs/MEMORY-PILLAR-DESIGN.md, "Increment 5: split-frontier drop
// flags" for the exact commands and results.
fn main() ~ Console {
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < 1000000 {
        if i % 2 == 0 {
            let s = i.to_string() + "!";
            if i % 4 == 0 {
                println!("a {}", s);
            } else {
                println!("b {}", s);
            }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
}
